")],.object [("term",.text "resonance"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/R05326")]]),("sources",.array [.text "PAC, 1994, 66, 1077. 'Glossary of terms used in physical organic chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994)' on page 1099 (https://doi.org/10.1351/pac199466051077)"]),("text",.text "In the original meaning a conjugated system is a molecular entity whose structure may be represented as a system of alternating single and multiple bonds: e.g. $\\ce{CH2=CH–CH=CH2}$, $\\ce{CH2=CH–C#N}$. In such systems, conjugation is the interaction of one p-orbital with another across an intervening $\\ce{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox{-}bond}$ in such structures. (In appropriate molecular entities d-orbitals may be involved.) The term is also extended to the analogous interaction involving a p-orbital containing an unshared electron pair, e.g. $\\ce{{:}Cl–CH=CH2}$.")]),("complete_disclaimer",.text "The International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) is continuously reviewing and, where needed, updating terms in the Compendium of Chemical Terminology (the IUPAC Gold Book). Users of these terms are encouraged to include the version of a term with its use and to check regularly for updates to term definitions that you are using."),("complete_licence",.text "The IUPAC Gold Book is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike CC BY-SA 4.0 International (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/) for individual terms."),("complete_synonym_surface",.text "<em>synonym</em>: conjugation"),("source_code",.text "C01267"),("source_status",.text "current"),("source_title",.text "conjugated system")]),("source_record_ordinal",.integer (1)),("source_record_role",.text "complete-conjugated-system-record"),("target_id",.text "SFT-CHEM-ORG-001-001"),("target_payload_hash",.text "sha256:5234ca5ee5bb618baa9826c863deea524c03823309bff29eab1c7ea2df5455c3")],.object [("authority",.text "IUPAC"),("custody_class",.text "family-development-observed"),("registered_identity",.text "pi-conjugated system complete current term record"),("snapshot_path",.text "experiments/external_sources/chemistry/snapshots/org-001-016-family-v1/iupac-08786.json"),("snapshot_sha256",.text "sha256:f9a6746d14f359927f1a38df5811c87770d759613250c5ff7800b56cdbbe1497"),("source_id",.text "IUPAC-08786"),("source_outcome",.object [("complete_citation",.text "Citation: 'π-conjugated system' in IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, 5th ed. International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry; 2025. Online version 5.0.0, 2025. 10.1351/goldbook.08786"),("complete_definition",.object [("id",.integer (1)),("links",.array [.object [("term",.text "conjugated polymers"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/08785")],.object [("term",.text "delocalization of electrons"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/08789")],.object [("term",.text "molecular orbital theory"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/08841")],.object [("term",.text "valence bond theory"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/08878")]]),("notes",.object [("1",.text "Examples of conjugated systems are conjugated polymers and molecule species whose formulas drawn in terms of the valence bond theory are as follows: "),("2",.text "Alternating arrangement of the single and multiple bonds allows the delocalization of electrons, which stabilizes conjugated systems. The most effective stabilization show planary cyclic systems with \\(2n + 2\\) delocalized electrons (where \\(n\\) is integer), so-called aromatic systems. The last two examples above are the species where the pair non-bonding electrons (thiophene) or electrons of negatively charged atom are delocalized together with \\(\\ce{\\uppi\\mbox{-}electrons}\\) giving an aromatic system."),("3",.text "Due to the delocalization of electrons a formula drawn in terms of the valence bond theory does not fully describe the structure of a conjugated species, which actually is a combination of two or more limiting or intermediate resonance structures that are in a dynamic equilibrium. The molecular orbital theory which directly provides extended molecular orbitals spread over all contributing atoms allows better understanding of the electronic and optical properties of a conjugated system.")]),("sources",.array [.text "PAC, 2022, 94, 15. 'Glossary of terms relating to electronic, photonic and magnetic properties of polymers (IUPAC Recommendations 2021)' on page 21 (https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2020-0501)"]),("text",.text "Molecule or a part of a molecule, of which structure may be represented as a system of alternating single and multiple bonds, in which, eventually, a multiple bond can be replaced by an atom with a pair of non-bonding electrons or an atom carrying a negative charge.")]),("complete_disclaimer",.text "The International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) is continuously reviewing and, where needed, updating terms in the Compendium of Chemical Terminology (the IUPAC Gold Book). Users of these terms are encouraged to include the version of a term with its use and to check regularly for updates to term definitions that you are using."),("complete_licence",.text "The IUPAC Gold Book is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike CC BY-SA 4.0 International (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/) for individual terms."),("complete_synonym_surface",.null),("source_code",.text "08786"),("source_status",.text "current"),("source_title",.text "π-conjugated system")]),("source_record_ordinal",.integer (2)),("source_record_role",.text "complete-pi-conjugated-system-record"),("target_id",.text "SFT-CHEM-ORG-001-002"),("target_payload_hash",.text "sha256:be9251c07763c67ff66c43c436964a95fb2c720e0a128c8b4624eb9dbccadfa2")],.object [("authority",.text "IUPAC"),("custody_class",.text "family-identity-only-unopened"),("registered_identity",.text "delocalization complete current term record"),("snapshot_path",.text "experiments/external_sources/chemistry/snapshots/org-001-016-family-v1/iupac-d01583.json"),("snapshot_sha256",.text "sha256:4a6ad1affe5c228511297827f6bd9ca2217697d9ff10089309369e924dfa3443"),("source_id",.text "IUPAC-D01583"),("source_outcome",.object [("complete_citation",.text "Citation: 'delocalization' in IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, 5th ed. International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry; 2025. Online version 5.0.0, 2025. 10.1351/goldbook.D01583"),("complete_definition",.object [("id",.integer (1)),("links",.array [.object [("term",.text "aromatic"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/A00441")],.object [("term",.text "bond order"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/B00707")],.object [("term",.text "cation"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/C00907")],.object [("term",.text "conjugated system"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/C01267")],.object [("term",.text "contributing structures"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/C01309")],.object [("term",.text "enamines"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/E02066")],.object [("term",.text "mesomerism"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/M03845")],.object [("term",.text "molecular entities"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/M03986")],.object [("term",.text "nitro compounds"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/N04158")],.object [("term",.text "radicals"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/R05066")],.object [("term",.text "resonance"),("url",.text "https://goldbook.iupac.org//terms/view/R05326")]]),("sources",.array [.text "PAC, 1994, 66, 1077. 'Glossary of terms used in physical organic chemistry (IUPAC Recommendations 1994)' on page 1104 (https://doi.org/10.1351/pac199466051077)"]),("text",.text "A quantum mechanical concept most usually applied in organic chemistry to describe the $\\ce{\\uppi\\!\\mbox{-}bonding}$ in a conjugated system. This bonding is not localized between two atoms: instead, each link has a 'fractional double bond character' or bond order. There is a corresponding 'delocalization energy', identifiable with the stabilization of the system compared with a hypothetical alternative in which formal (localized) single and double bonds are present. Some degree of delocalization is always present and can be estimated by quantum mechanical calculations. The effects are particularly evident in aromatic systems and in symmetrical molecular entities in which a lone pair of electrons or a vacant p-orbital is conjugated with a double bond (e.g. carboxylate ions, nitro compounds, enamines, the allyl cation). Delocalization in such species may be represented by partial bonds or as resonance (here symbolized by a two-headed arrow) between contributing structures.   These examples also illustrate the concomitant delocalization of charge in ionic conjugated systems. Analogously, delocalization of the spin of an unpaired electron occurs in conjugated radicals.")]),("complete_disclaimer",.text "The International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) is continuously reviewing and, where needed, updating terms in the Compendium of Chemical Terminology (the IUPAC Gold Book). Users of these terms are encouraged to include the version of a term with its use and to check regularly for updates to term definitions that you are using."),("complete_licence",.text "The IUPAC Gold Book is licensed under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike CC BY-SA 4.0 International (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/) for individual terms."),("complete_synonym_surface",.null),("source_code",.text "D01583"),("source_status",.text "current"),("source_title",.text "delocalization")]),("source_record_ordinal",.integer (3)),("source_record_role",.text "complete-delocalization-record"),("target_id",.text "SFT-CHEM-ORG-001-003"),("target_payload_hash",.text "sha256:57b6551d05d40e0b38a94e1dfa2744e2a9449d135ca00e1a486aeca23b11